<BaseLayout title="Mapping Educational Access | Student Mobility & Transportation" description="An original 2018 student-mobility research case study exploring transportation access, school stability and policy in Guilford County, North Carolina.">
<article class="gis-exhibit">

<header><div class="gis-container"><a class="gis-back" href="/research-writing/">← Research &amp; Writing</a><div class="kicker">Field research / High Point, North Carolina / 2018</div><h1>Mapping<br><em>Educational Access.</em></h1><p>How bus routes, school boundaries, and frequent moves affected students’ ability to stay at the same school.</p></div></header>
<section><div class="gis-container"><div class="kicker">01 / Research question</div><h2>A change of address shouldn't automatically mean a change of school.</h2><p>Our collaborative research examined student transiency in Guilford County, North Carolina, with particular attention to Fairview Elementary School. We looked at how bus routes, areas without school transportation, attendance boundaries, and reassignment rules affected children whose families moved frequently.</p><p class="citation">Historical context: this exhibit documents the 2018 research period rather than current transportation or district policy.</p></div></section>
<section class="dark"><div class="gis-container"><div class="kicker">02 / Transportation geography</div><h2>Two schools. Different routes.</h2><p>We compared Fairview and Oak Hill Elementary School bus routes, including school locations, attendance and non-transportation zones, stop sequences, and route timings. The digital maps below are the original completed project figures.</p><div class="pair"><figure><a href="/images/mapping-educational-access/fairview-digital-map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/mapping-educational-access/fairview-digital-map.png" width="512" height="256" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Fairview original digital bus map with colored bus route markings"></a><figcaption><b>01 — Fairview Elementary</b>Five morning buses operating for approximately 3–19 minutes. The red area represents the walk zone; blue marks the attendance area. Route symbols encode stop order.</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/mapping-educational-access/oak-hill-digital-map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/mapping-educational-access/oak-hill-digital-map.png" width="512" height="257" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Oak Hill original digital map showing buses and attendance zone"></a><figcaption><b>02 — Oak Hill Elementary</b>Seven morning buses operating for approximately 14–28 minutes. The blue area marks the non-transport zone and the red shading marks the attendance zone.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="kicker">Research process</div><h3>From hand-traced routes to digital analysis</h3><p>Original working maps recorded bus identification, street sequences, travel times, and stop observations. These annotations were the starting point for the final maps.</p><div class="pair"><figure><a href="/images/mapping-educational-access/fairview-working-map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/mapping-educational-access/fairview-working-map.jpg" width="1136" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Hand-drawn colored Fairview bus routes and written times"></a><figcaption><b>03 — Fairview working map</b>Annotated routes and bus timing notes.</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/mapping-educational-access/oak-hill-working-map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/mapping-educational-access/oak-hill-working-map.jpg" width="2047" height="1535" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Hand-drawn colored Oak Hill bus routes and written times"></a><figcaption><b>04 — Oak Hill working map</b>Annotated street segments and stop sequences.</figcaption></figure></div><p class="citation">Original map imagery includes Google Maps © 2018 Google. Route annotations come from the original project materials.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="gis-container"><div class="kicker">03 / Findings</div><h2>What the maps revealed.</h2><div class="notes"><div><strong>5</strong>Fairview morning buses<small>3–19-minute trips</small></div><div><strong>7</strong>Oak Hill morning buses<small>14–28-minute trips</small></div><div><strong>2018</strong>Bus-route analysis<small>Based on 2018 records</small></div></div><p>We found several routes whose stop order seemed inefficient. For example, an Oak Hill bus picked up students living geographically closer to Fairview Elementary than to Oak Hill. We wanted to know whether different routes or school assignments could make better use of buses while helping students stay at their schools.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="gis-container"><div class="kicker">04 / Research methods</div><h2>From records to recommendations.</h2><p>The collaborative research drew on historical mobility rates, transportation information, school staff perspectives, county transportation leadership interviews, route comparisons, and school-reassignment policy review.</p><div class="kicker" style="margin-top:55px">05 / Proposed interventions</div><h2>What we recommended.</h2><p><b>Transportation:</b> explore alternative routing, cross-school transportation, and shuttle concepts. <b>Non-transportation areas:</b> reevaluate accessibility and safety. <b>Policy access:</b> make reassignment information easier for families to identify and use. These were recommendations, not claims of implemented transportation changes.</p></div></section>
<section class="archive"><div class="gis-container"><div class="kicker">06 / Documented developments</div><h2>A district response later in 2018.</h2><p>On October 17, 2018, <em>The High Point Enterprise</em> reported that Guilford County Schools had issued a revised student-assignment procedure, effective in late September. The procedure allowed certain economically disadvantaged and highly mobile families to keep students at their current schools after moving outside their original attendance area.</p><p>The article names Fairview Elementary and describes the district’s effort to keep students from having to switch schools when their families moved. It does not say our research led to the change.</p><div class="newspaper"><figure><a href="/images/mapping-educational-access/high-point-enterprise-front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/mapping-educational-access/high-point-enterprise-front.jpg" width="1920" height="1486" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Newspaper original article page"></a><figcaption>Article front page — click to enlarge.</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/mapping-educational-access/high-point-enterprise-continuation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/mapping-educational-access/high-point-enterprise-continuation.jpg" width="1920" height="1486" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Newspaper continuation of article"></a><figcaption>Article continuation — click to enlarge.</figcaption></figure></div><p class="citation">Holly West, “Officials ease rules for transient family students,” <em>The High Point Enterprise</em>, October 17, 2018. Newspaper scans from the original research archive.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="gis-container"><div class="kicker">07 / Reflection</div><h2>Research that informs decisions.</h2><p>We started with a question about why students were changing schools so often and followed it through bus routes, interviews, attendance boundaries, and district policy. I still value that kind of research: finding out how a system works in practice, where it falls short, and what could change.</p><div class="foot"><b>Original research team:</b> Avery Moon, Makenzie Brockel, and Andrew DeHaan. <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KeTJMsTOoNjhKQlq59QnPNtKf8sn7EyNmcALfhtCLd4/edit">Original white paper</a> · <a href="https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1U7_ELkfh1JOkUyO0tvBsDhEUP0bZ-VmJGdColAPDRw0/edit">Presentation</a><p><a href="/research-writing/">← Return to Research &amp; Writing</a></p></div></div></section>

</article>
